<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (The focus group itself used photographs of different substances, short videos and agree-or-disagree statements to encourage participation. The Salik Project UK also uses images, video, lived-experience testimony and a drug-awareness box containing examples of unfamiliar packaging and equipment. These methods allow people to ask questions, challenge assumptions and recognise items that may otherwise go unnoticed.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (Waffa Hameed, one of the facilitators, shared how returning to prayer had become an important foundation in his recovery from alcohol dependency and in rebuilding his relationships with his wife and children. His experience also showed that faith formed part of a wider recovery journey rather than replacing professional or practical support.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (Participants felt that occasional talks from visiting speakers were not enough. They wanted addiction and substance misuse to become a regular part of preventative education, potentially once or twice each month. Muslim children may spend around two hours a day, five days a week, attending mosque or madrassah. Participants saw this as a major opportunity to reach children before harmful behaviour begins. They also believed that when one mosque demonstrated effective work, others were more likely to follow.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (Imams should have basic awareness of addiction and know where people can obtain professional help. However, their main responsibility is religious instruction and the running of the mosque. They cannot reasonably be expected to become specialist addiction workers as well.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (Participants also noted how rare it was to encounter Muslim or South Asian people working within recovery services. Having access to somebody who understood their family, faith and community context was seen as valuable, particularly when a person was already afraid of being judged.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
