170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (A generational gap also ran through the discussion. Parents may care deeply but lack the time, knowledge or confidence to discuss drugs before a problem develops. Threats and punishment can make young people more secretive, while shame, izzat and fear of gossip can delay families from seeking help.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (The discussion also showed that the method of engagement matters. Lived experience, images, video, practical activities and open conversation held greater value than generic information delivered without depth or enthusiasm. Young people wanted education that reflected the substances, pressures and online environments they actually encountered.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Above all, the participants wanted action rather than another conversation that disappeared. Their contributions point towards current drug education, practical support for parents, basic awareness for imams, paid grassroots leadership, positive activities, clearer routes into help and visible follow-up from the organisations asking communities to share their experiences.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
